
  <sn><md>### Cation Exchange

A **cation exchange reaction in soil** is the `reversible process` by which positively charged ions (cations) held on the `surfaces` of soil particles are replaced by other cations from the `soil solution`. It is one of the most important chemical processes governing soil `fertility and nutrient availability`.

**The Basic Reaction**

$$
\text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + 2\text{K}^+ \rightleftharpoons \text{Soil} - 2\text{K}^+ + \text{Ca}^{2+}
$$

A cation adsorbed on the `soil surface` (here Ca²⁺) is exchanged with a cation in `solution` (here K⁺). The reaction is `reversible` and reaches `equilibrium` depending on the relative concentrations and binding strengths of the ions.
</md></sn>
